ry":"media-entertainment","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Changes to NCAA eligibility rules could affect the composition and marketability of collegiate sports programming and events."}],"plainSummary":"This resolution calls on the National Collegiate Athletic Association to withdraw its current policy allowing transgender student-athletes to compete and instead adopt rules requiring participation in women's sports to be based on biological sex. The measure urges the NCAA to mandate that all member conferences implement such a policy across every sport and division. As a resolution, it expresses the position of the House but does not create enforceable law.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"College students, and their families.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"H001096","score":0.005818742346366307},"overlapCount":11},"meaning":{"percentile":11.941946679286954,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.8743718687934968}},{"billId":"119-hr-2705","bioguideId":"M000194","cycle":2026,"score":0.0023644822014967214,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":815,"donorShare":0.003422829474635574,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.0021781642111317286},{"industry":"hospitality","donorAmount":122,"donorShare":0.00051237447350373,"billConfidence":0.4,"contribution":0.00018631799036499276}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.8743718687934968},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-2705","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"2705","title":"Nuclear Family Priority Act","introducedDate":"2025-04-08","latestActionDate":"2025-04-08","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on the Judiciary.","policyArea":"Immigration","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"C001132","cosponsorCount":45,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/2705","industries":[{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Immigration law firms would see changes in demand for services related to family-based visa petitions and the new nonimmigrant parent visa category."},{"industry":"hospitality","confidence":0.4,"rationale":"Reduced family immigration could affect demand for travel and lodging services related to family visits and relocation."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would sharply reduce the number of family-based immigration visas available each year, lowering the annual cap from 480,000 to 88,000. It would eliminate visa categories for siblings and married children of U.S. citizens, and would remove parents of citizens from the immediate-relative category that currently has no numerical limit. Parents of citizens would instead be eligible for a temporary visa that does not allow them to work or receive public benefits.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People applying to live here, and their families here.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"K000405","score":0.03341706325741149},"overlapCount":41},"meaning":{"percentile":10.42751222590314,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.8743718687934968}},{"billId":"119-hr-29","bioguideId":"M000194","cycle":2026,"score":0.0016625171733944217,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":815,"donorShare":0.003422829474635574,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.0016625171733944217}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.8743718687934968},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-29","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"29","title":"Laken Riley Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionDate":"2025-02-10","latestActionText":"Read the second time. Placed on Senate Legislative Calendar under General Orders. Calendar No. 10.","policyArea":"Immigration","subjects":["Border security and unlawful immigration","Civil actions and liability","Crimes against property","Criminal investigation, prosecution, interrogation","Detention of persons","Immigration status and procedures","Intergovernmental relations","Refugees, asylum, displaced persons","State and local government operations","Visas and passports"],"sponsorBioguideId":"C001129","cosponsorCount":53,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/29","industries":[{"industry":"government","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill directly imposes new detention mandates on DHS and creates new litigation exposure for federal agencies, affecting federal and state government operations and budgets."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill creates new grounds for state litigation against the federal government and expands detention requirements, increasing demand for legal services in immigration and intergovernmental litigation."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would require the Department of Homeland Security to detain individuals who are in the country without authorization and have been arrested for or charged with burglary, theft, larceny, or shoplifting. It would also allow state governments to sue the federal government for injunctive relief when federal immigration enforcement decisions or failures—such as releasing individuals from custody or not fulfilling visa or asylum processing requirements—cause harm to the state or its residents, including financial harm exceeding $100.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People applying to live here, and their families here. People applying to come here, and their families here.","confide